funnel_seen        = packets entering the L2 funnel
funnel_suppressed  = packets intentionally rejected by 30-sec dedup
funnel_shipped     = packets accepted by dedup and sent onward
live_dump_dropped  = packets that passed dedup but couldn't enter liveDumpQueue
leak_isr_dropped   = crypto/deauth packets that couldn't enter leakQueue directly
leak_core1_dropped = parsed packets that couldn't enter leakQueue on Core 1


header counters and the display funnel

The header at the top of the real-time screen shows three numbers, for example
`12/239/251`. They form a funnel — the intended relationship is x <= y <= z — and
each is the delta over the last ~3-second telemetry window, so the values give
an immediate indication of current RF activity rather than lifetime totals.

Under the hood, `pcap_displayed_total`, `pcap_upstream_total`, and
`pcap_cooldown_total` are cumulative accounting counters for the current
telemetry session and preserve the x <= y <= z invariant; the header renders the
per-window change in each. Session and mode resets re-baseline the window
snapshots safely. The semantics of each stage:

    x — leaks logged/displayed. Distinct leak results that made it through
    processing and were written to the serial log (a unique source-MAC + text
    pairing). It is not the number of rows drawn on the LCD, despite the "made
    it to screen" phrasing used during development.

    y — successfully enqueued. Events actually placed into a queue, including
    the deauth and crypto/WEP-TKIP bypass paths.

    z — accepted into the pipeline. Events that passed their applicable
    acceptance/cooldown gate and proceeded toward queueing, again including the
    bypass paths. A queue-full event therefore counts toward z but not y; the
    gap y-to-z measures frames accepted but never enqueued.

The first number can reach 100+ per cycle while the screen can only ever show a
handful of rows. That is expected and healthy. Every distinct leak flows into
three separate places, and only the serial log keeps all of them:

    1. Serial monitor — keeps everything. Each distinct leak prints one CLRTXT
    line. Nothing is lost here; this is the complete record.

    2. Real-time waterfall — keeps the most recent 5. It is a rolling window:
    each new distinct leak pushes the oldest one off the bottom. Over a busy
    cycle it scrolls through 100+ distinct leaks, but at any instant only the
    latest five are visible.

    3. Persistent (DEVICES) list — keeps at most 20, by score. Entries are
    retained by a score (text length plus a high-value bonus). Of the 100+
    distinct leaks in a busy cycle, only about 20 earn or keep a slot; the rest
    are never inserted or are later evicted by a higher-value newcomer.

So nothing is silently dropped from the pipeline: every distinct leak is
serial-printed. The two on-screen views are small and curated — a live "tail"
of five and a "best-of" list of twenty — while serial remains the authoritative
mirror.


probe request tracking

When a device scans for networks, it broadcasts probe request frames announcing
the network names (SSIDs) it is looking for. These frames reveal a device's
history — every network it has previously joined — which makes them rich
identification evidence. Wavehound tracks this in its probe tracker view.

Randomized MACs. Modern phones and laptops randomize the transmitter MAC address
in probe requests (the locally-administered bit is set on the address), rotating
to a new identity periodically so the same phone appears as many different
"devices". Wavehound detects this (`mac[0] & 0x02`) and applies two rules:

    - A physical (non-randomized) MAC always passes, and its vendor is resolved
    from the OUI database.
    - A randomized MAC passes only if the probe carries a real, printable SSID —
    a randomized probe for a wildcard (hidden-network) sweep carries no useful
    identity, so it is not tracked.

For randomized MACs, the vendor shown is inferred from Vendor Specific
information elements (IE tag 221) in the probe itself when present (e.g.
"Apple~IE"); otherwise it falls to SD-card OUI resolution of the transient
address, which for randomized MACs usually resolves to the chip maker rather
than the phone brand.

IE-based device fingerprinting (hardware hash). Every tracked probe builds a
32-bit fingerprint from the probe's information elements:

    - The tag numbers of every IE present are hashed in order, capturing the
    structural skeleton of the frame (which capabilities the chipset advertises).
    - The complete payload bytes of three relatively stable IEs are also hashed:
    tag 45 (HT Capabilities), tag 127 (Extended Capabilities), and tag 221
    (Vendor Specific).

This is best understood as an implementation fingerprint rather than a true
hardware identity: it fingerprints the chipset/driver's IE composition. It is
strict equality — a device that omits or reorders an information element
between probes can produce different hashes for the same radio, so it is one
signal, not proof.

PNL (Preferred Network List) evidence. Each tracked device accumulates two
forms of "where has this device been" evidence:

    - Exact SSID history — the real strings, deduplicated, in a per-device list
    (up to 15 entries, drawn from a shared 150-slot pool).
    - A 64-bit bitmap (`pnl_hash`) — each SSID folds to one bit via a djb2 hash
    mod 64. This is cheap and mergeable, but lossy: two different SSIDs collide
    into the same bit about 1 time in 64, so the bitmap is a hint, not proof.
    Wildcard probes (`<Wld>` for broadcast sweeps, `<Nul>` for blank/hidden
    names) are recorded but deliberately excluded from the bitmap.

Smart endpoint identification (correlation). Because a rotating MAC makes one
phone look like five, the tracker runs a background correlation pass (every
~5 s) that tries to merge records that are probably the same physical device.
Two records are compared with:

    1. Hardware gate — the IE fingerprints (above) must match exactly.
    Mismatched fingerprints are never merged.
    2. Network evidence — the two devices' PNL bitmaps are compared. An
    identical non-empty bitmap scores +50; a subset/superset relationship
    scores +30, but only when both devices have actually probed networks and
    the exact SSID histories share at least one real network name (empty
    bitmaps and bitmap-only coincidences score nothing).
    3. Spatial evidence — instantaneous RSSI within 5 dB scores +40; more than
    15 dB apart subtracts 50.

A merge needs a total of 70+ points. On a merge, the newer MAC and timestamps
are adopted, hit counters and MAC-rotation counts accumulate, and the exact
SSID lists are unioned — so a device that has rotated MACs twice shows one row
with 3 rotations and the combined network history, instead of three separate
entries.

What correlation cannot do: it cannot merge a rotated MAC whose fingerprint
changed between probes (the hard gate rejects it), and it cannot rescue two
devices with identical chipsets that probe identical networks at similar signal
strength — those are genuinely ambiguous from the air.


supported protocols

You now have parsers for things including:

DHCPv4 / DHCPv6
DNS/mDNS
ARP
ICMPv4 / ICMPv6
EAPOL
CDP / LLDP
NetBIOS / NBDS
SSDP / UPnP
HTTP
WS-Discovery
IPP
FTP
Telnet
SSH
SMTP
SMB
RTSP
SNMP
MQTT
TFTP
RDP
TLS certificate/SNI extraction
DropBox discovery traffic

And we specifically added/discussed SOCKS/SOCKS5.

Protocol candidates (considered, not yet implemented)

The most useful remaining candidates, in my view:

NTP — very cheap parser; useful for identifying time servers and NTP clients.
LDAP — potentially valuable enterprise/device identity information.
Kerberos — strong enterprise/network identity signals, although more complex.
mDNS/LLMNR enhancements — particularly richer hostname/service extraction if your existing parsers are still minimal.
DHCP option expansion — vendor class, client identifier, hostname, domain search list, etc., if you haven't already incorporated all of these.
SIP — useful for VoIP discovery and user/device information.
CoAP — particularly worth doing if you're interested in IoT; you've currently got a static port fingerprint rather than a real parser.
STUN/TURN — you've got a STUN magic-cookie signature, but a structured parser could extract transaction/mapping information.
SSDP/UPnP enrichment — you already parse it, but there is room to extract more device/service metadata.

no stream reassembly

TCP SYN (0x02): This is a connection request. If someone on the network is running a stealth port scan (like nmap -sS), they send thousands of SYN packets to various ports without ever actually completing the connection or sending data. Catching these alerts you immediately that a device is mapping the network.

TCP RST (0x04): This is a connection reset/rejection. If you see a flurry of RST packets, it usually means a device is trying to talk to a closed port, or a firewall/Intrusion Detection System (IDS) is actively terminating an unauthorized connection.


Known limitations

Because Wave Hound operates on a stateless, packet-by-packet basis, there are three scenarios on the modern internet where these parsers will intentionally fail to extract text:

    The TCP Boundary Guillotine: If a TCP fragment splits an ASN.1 header perfectly in half (e.g., the 0x06 tag is the last byte of Packet A, and the 0x03 length is the first byte of Packet B), the sliding window will miss it. This is normal and acceptable for your hardware constraints.

    The 127-Byte ASN.1 Limit: As your friend pointed out, if a single element inside a certificate (like a massive list of 50 Subject Alternative Names) exceeds 127 bytes, it uses a "long-form" length flag (0x81 or 0x82). Our parser assumes short-form lengths to save CPU cycles. It will safely skip over these massive blocks, meaning you might occasionally miss a domain name if it is buried inside a giant SAN block.

    Encrypted Client Hello (ECH): Cloudflare and Chrome are currently rolling out ECH, which encrypts the SNI extension so middleboxes cannot read it. When Wave Hound captures an ECH packet, it will correctly parse the TLS headers, but it will only see the "outer" cover domain (usually something generic like cloudflare-ech.com) rather than the true target.

Overall, the logic is highly robust and ready to tackle random network traffic.


eapol
What is the MIC?

MIC stands for Message Integrity Code. Once the Access Point (AP) and the Station (STA - the client device) combine the network password, the AP's MAC, the STA's MAC, the ANonce (from M1), and the SNonce (from M2), they generate a master session key called the PTK (Pairwise Transient Key).

A piece of that PTK is the Key Confirmation Key (KCK). The MIC is an HMAC (a cryptographic hash) calculated by feeding the entire EAPOL frame + the KCK into an algorithm (usually SHA-1 or AES-CMAC).
Why M2, M3, and M4 have different MICs:

Because the MIC is a hash of the frame's contents, any change to the payload completely changes the resulting MIC.

    M1 (AP -> STA): Has no MIC. The AP is just handing the client the ANonce so the client can do the math to build the keys.

    M2 (STA -> AP): The client builds the keys, generates the SNonce, and sends it to the AP. It hashes this specific packet with the KCK to create MIC 1 (F92C...).

    M3 (AP -> STA): The AP verifies M2. Now it needs to send the client the encrypted Group Key (GTK) for broadcast traffic. It increments the "Key Replay Counter" (so hackers can't replay old packets). Because the payload now contains the encrypted GTK and a new replay counter, hashing it produces a completely different signature: MIC 2 (6EB6...).

    M4 (STA -> AP): The client sends a final, tiny packet that is basically just an "Ack" confirming it installed the keys. It has no nonces or key data, just a new replay counter. Different payload = MIC 3 (5801...).

If the MIC were identical across packets, a hacker could simply copy the MIC from M2 and forge their own M3 or M4 packets to hijack the handshake!


channel timing: window, sweep, and dwell

Every hopping mode (WIFI, NETWORKS, CHANNELS, PCAP, CT) shares one timing rule, derived from the selected channel count N (the SELECT CH set, or all 13 channels when no set is configured):

    k      = max(1, floor(5000 ms / (N x 300 ms)))   sweeps per window
    dwell  = round(5000 ms / (k x N))                per-channel airtime
    window = k x N x dwell                           ~= 5000 ms for every N

The dwell never drops below the 300 ms floor (enough airtime to catch beacon frames, which fire every ~102 ms). Worked examples: 13 channels give k=1, dwell=385 ms, window=5005 ms; 3 channels give k=5, dwell=333 ms, window=4995 ms; 2 channels give k=8, dwell=313 ms, window=5008 ms.

Three clocks are involved, and they are deliberately distinct:

    Dwell clock — how long the radio sits on one channel before hopping. Varies with N; the first fire of a scan always lands on the lowest channel of the set (mode switches and scan starts re-arm the sweep there).

    Sweep clock — one complete pass over the selected channels (N x dwell: 999 ms at 3 channels, ~5 s at 13). The sweep has no statistical meaning; it is just the radio's rotation period.

    Window clock — the ~5 s observation window. In CT mode this is the statistical window that segmentation consumes (one window = k complete sweeps; a window closing off-screen or mid-stall is discarded). The waterfall bars in every hopping mode also advance on ~5 s boundaries: CT advances one column per window close; the other modes advance one column at the first sweep boundary after ~5 s, so bars always land on the same channel (the set's lowest) and the visible time scale never depends on N.

N therefore changes only the sampling pattern (how airtime is divided and how often each channel is revisited — every channel receives exactly k visits per window), never the visible time scale or the meaning of the statistics. Blocked-loop moments (e.g. an SD vendor lookup) may stretch a window's real-time span slightly; windows always close on complete sweeps.


Chase Tail (CT) workflow

CHASE TAIL is the countersurveillance mode: it detects devices that persist
across changing RF environments. This section describes the current
implementation; the design history and engineering rationale are in Chase Tail
Notes.md. The mode is Wi-Fi-based; BLE device correlation is a planned
extension and is not part of the current behavior.

Observation windows. CT operates on ~5 s statistical windows (see "channel
timing" above for how the window is derived from the selected channel count —
the window is NOT a radio sweep; one window spans k complete sweeps). Each
window accumulates the identities observed on-air: BSSIDs (access points) and
SSIDs (network names, including names announced in probe requests). A window
that closed while the processing loop was paused (e.g. during display or SD
work) is invalid and contributes nothing to the statistics; zero-observation
windows are real observations and do count.

Environment tracking. Committed environments are kept in a ring (E0, E1, ...).
A commit copies the open window's accumulated identity sets into the ring,
clears the accumulator, and re-seeds all statistics, so each new segment starts
from a clean, seeded state. The RF ENVS screen lists the committed environments
with their first/last seen wall-clock times and window counts.

Evidence channels and segmentation. Environment changes are detected by three
independent evidence channels, each answering a different question:

    POPULATION — is the observed number of identities normal, compared to a
    baseline?

    CHURN — is the per-window flux (how much the identity counts change) larger
    than baseline?

    TURNOVER — are identities themselves being swapped in and out (new arrivals
    plus vanished identities, plus inserts that had to be rejected because the
    accumulator tables were full — the only sensor of the wider environment
    when the tables are saturated)?

Each channel maintains a horizon-bounded EWMA baseline (population mean and
noise on one shared clock, churn/turnover flux means), seeded from the first
observations of each segment. Per-window divergence is converted to evidence,
smoothed, and a channel "triggers" after sustained evidence. A commit requires
a 2-of-3 quorum of triggering channels. Baselines re-seed at commit, so
re-entering an already-learned environment produces little or no evidence.

Persistent-device tracking. The payload of a commit is persistence: a device
identity (MAC) that is observed in a second distinct committed environment is
promoted to CT's persistent-device table — the classic "same device keeps
showing up in different places" signature. The table is capacity-limited and
evicts by last-seen time. Probe-request traffic and SSID identity information
associated with a persistent MAC enrich its record.

Display and channel selection. CT's waterfall advances one column per window
close (~5 s), so the visible time scale is independent of the selected channel
count. The traffic band under the waterfall shows the per-window byte delta —
every received frame of at least 24 bytes (signalled length including FCS) that
arrived during the window, zero-rolled and conserved across window boundaries —
so its magnitude is comparable to the per-interval traffic charts in the other
hopping modes. Changing the channel set in CT (SELECT CH) is deferred to a safe window
boundary — the new set is applied at the next window close rather than mid-window,
so windows remain composed of complete sweeps. A window closing off-screen or
during a long processing stall is discarded by the validity rule.
